_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/588"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "100 metres Freestyle, Women" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "29 November –  1 December 1956"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/402"} "Olympic Park Swimming and Diving Stadium, Melbourne, Victoria"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/5441"} "35 from 16 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "For 20 years the time set in early 1936 by the Dutch swimmer " [:a {:href "/athletes/49321"} "Willy den Ouden"] " remained unchallenged as the world record.  Once broken by 18-year-old Australian " [:a {:href "/athletes/45044"} "Dawn Fraser"] " in February 1956 it was broken over and over again in the next eight months by a trio of teenagers. " [:a {:href "/athletes/49409"} "Cocki van Engelsdorp Gastelaars"] " held the record for the Northern hemisphere summer, Fraser regained it before the youngest of three, 17-year-old " [:a {:href "/athletes/45020"} "Lorraine Crapp"] ", set a new mark at the Australian selection trials. The stage was set for the three women to produce an epic race in Melbourne but it would not fully take place as Gastelaars was a victim of the Dutch boycott of the 1956 Games.\nIn the absence of the Dutchwoman the event turned into a bonanza for the host nation of Australia."] "\n\n" [:p {} "As expected, Fraser and Crapp contested the gold medal with the pair forcing each other to go faster than they’d ever gone before in pursuit of the title. Both swam inside the existing world record but it was Fraser who was crowned Olympic champion. " [:a {:href "/athletes/45073"} "Faith Leech"] ", at only 15 years of age, completed an Australian sweep of the medals, which matched what happened in the men’s equivalent race 24 hours earlier.\nThis was the first of a sequence of 3 successive championships in t